{
  "early_exit_signal": "mismatch_found (registered in the always @(posedge clk) block of signature_compare.v; when running && !done && byte_valid and expected_byte != received_byte, the RTL executes `mismatch_found <= 1'b1; done <= 1'b1; match <= 1'b0;` on that very clock edge, terminating the comparison immediately at the first differing byte pair instead of continuing to consume the remaining bytes)",
  "cycle_relationship": "Let k be the 0-indexed byte position of the first mismatching byte pair (0..15), using the testbench_harness.v convention where cycle_count is incremented once per @(posedge clk) starting the cycle after the start pulse. On the cycle in which byte position i is presented with byte_valid=1, the module registers done<=1 (and mismatch_found<=1, match<=0) the same edge if expected_byte != received_byte, and this registered done value is observed as asserted on the following sampled cycle_count increment consistent with the harness's per-cycle counting. Empirically/derivationally: cycles_to_done = k + 2 for a mismatch at position k (k=0 -> 2 cycles, k=1 -> 3 cycles, ..., k=15 -> 17 cycles). For a full match (no mismatching byte), the terminal equality check `byte_count == 15` fires only when the 16th byte (index 15) is consumed, asserting done on that edge with match<=1; this yields cycles_to_done = 18 for the full-match case. Thus cycles_to_done is a strictly increasing, data-dependent function of the first-mismatch position: earlier mismatches finish faster (as few as 2 cycles), later mismatches take longer (up to 17 cycles), and a full match takes the longest at 18 cycles. This monotonic relationship between input data and timing is the core side channel: an attacker who can distinguish these cycle counts (directly or via a correlated physical side channel such as power/EM) can determine, byte by byte, how many leading bytes of a guessed signature match the expected one, enabling a byte-at-a-time forgery attack in O(256*16) trials.",
  "constant_time_fix": "Remove the early-exit branch entirely: the code path `if (expected_byte != received_byte) begin mismatch_found <= 1'b1; done <= 1'b1; match <= 1'b0; end` must no longer assert done on the mismatching cycle. Instead, restructure the running/!done branch so that on every cycle where byte_valid is high (regardless of whether the current byte pair matches), the module (1) does NOT terminate early on any mismatch, (2) unconditionally advances byte_count for all 16 byte positions so that all 16 bytes are always consumed before done can assert, and (3) accumulates a sticky/latched mismatch flag every cycle via `mismatch_found <= mismatch_found | (expected_byte != received_byte)` (a bitwise OR of the per-byte inequality into the persistent mismatch_found register, rather than an immediate branch that short-circuits consumption). Only after byte_count reaches its terminal value of 15 (i.e., the 16th byte pair has been consumed) should the module assert `done <= 1'b1; match <= ~mismatch_found;`. With this change, cycles_to_done is fixed at 18 cycles for every input (full match, mismatch at any of the 16 positions), independent of where or whether a mismatch occurs, eliminating the data-dependent timing channel while producing identical match/mismatch verdicts to the original design.",
  "baseline_behavior": {
    "full_match_verdict": "match",
    "mismatch_at_zero_verdict": "mismatch"
  },
  "proposed_fix_preserves_correctness": true
}
